<h1>Application integration (OIDC)</h1>
<p class="lede">Booster Auth is a standard OpenID Connect provider. Any library that supports OIDC works; this page lists the values you need and
    examples for common platforms.</p>

<h2 id="values">Values you need</h2>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Issuer (authority)</td><td><code>https://auth.company.com/idp</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Discovery</td><td><code>https://auth.company.com/idp/.well-known/openid-configuration</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>client_id</td><td>The application's client key, e.g. <code>acme-web</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>redirect_uri</td><td>A URI registered on the application, e.g. <code>https://app.company.com/callback</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Flow</td><td>Authorization Code + PKCE (<code>S256</code> required; the implicit flow is not supported)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scopes</td><td><code>openid profile email offline_access</code> + custom scopes allowed for the application. A custom scope is put in the
            token only if the <b>user holds it</b>; scopes granted to the user are added even if not requested.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>resource (optional)</td><td>The target API (audience) of the token, e.g. <code>https://api.company.com</code>; it must be one of the
            application's allowed APIs. Without it the audience is the IdP itself (<code>https://auth.company.com/idp</code>); that token works with <code>/idp/me</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>prompt (optional)</td><td><code>login</code>: sign in again even with a session. <code>create</code>: sign-up screen. <code>none</code>: no screen
            is shown; without a session the app gets <code>error=login_required</code>, if consent is needed <code>error=consent_required</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>max_age (optional)</td><td>Seconds. If the user's last sign-in is older, sign-in is required again; <code>auth_time</code> in the
            ID token is the real sign-in time.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<div class="callout"><p><b>One issuer.</b> The issuer is the same for all pools (<code>/idp</code>); the pool a user signs in to is determined by
    the pool the <code>client_id</code> belongs to.</p></div>

<h2 id="endpoints">Endpoints</h2>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Endpoint</th><th>Address</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Authorization</td><td><code>/idp/authorize</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Token</td><td><code>/idp/token</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>JWKS (signing keys, RS256)</td><td><code>/idp/.well-known/jwks.json</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>User info</td><td><code>/idp/me</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Logout</td><td><code>/idp/logout</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="spa">SPA / JavaScript</h2>
<p>This example uses the popular <code>oidc-client-ts</code> library; no client secret lives in the browser and PKCE is applied automatically.</p>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">JavaScript</span><pre>import { UserManager } from "oidc-client-ts";

const auth = new UserManager({
  authority: "https://auth.company.com/idp",
  client_id: "acme-web",
  redirect_uri: "https://app.company.com/callback",
  post_logout_redirect_uri: "https://app.company.com/",
  response_type: "code",
  scope: "openid profile email offline_access",
});

// Sign in
await auth.signinRedirect();

// On the /callback page
const user = await auth.signinRedirectCallback();
console.log(user.profile.email, user.access_token);</pre></div>

<h2 id="dotnet">ASP.NET Core (server-side web)</h2>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">C#</span><pre>builder.Services
    .AddAuthentication(o =>
    {
        o.DefaultScheme = CookieAuthenticationDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
        o.DefaultChallengeScheme = OpenIdConnectDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
    })
    .AddCookie()
    .AddOpenIdConnect(o =>
    {
        o.Authority = "https://auth.company.com/idp";
        o.ClientId = "acme-web";
        o.ResponseType = "code";
        o.UsePkce = true;
        o.Scope.Add("email");
        o.MapInboundClaims = false;    // keep "sub", "email", "role" as is
    });</pre></div>

<h2 id="api">Protecting your API</h2>
<p>Your API validates incoming access tokens with the issuer's JWKS keys. The token's <code>aud</code> is the API address the client requested as
    <code>resource</code>.</p>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">C# — Web API</span><pre>builder.Services
    .AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddJwtBearer(o =>
    {
        o.Authority = "https://auth.company.com/idp";
        o.Audience = "https://api.company.com";
        o.MapInboundClaims = false;
    });

app.MapGet("/orders", () => "...")
   .RequireAuthorization(p => p.RequireClaim("scope", "orders:read"));</pre></div>

<h2 id="tokens">Token contents</h2>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Claims</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Access token</td><td><code>sub</code>, <code>aud</code>, <code>azp</code>, <code>scope</code>, <code>role</code> (one per role), <code>sid</code>, <code>principal_type</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>ID token (only if <code>openid</code> is requested)</td><td><code>sub</code>, <code>nonce</code>, <code>auth_time</code>; <code>name</code> only with
            <code>profile</code>, <code>email</code> and <code>email_verified</code> only with the <code>email</code> scope</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/idp/me</code></td><td><code>sub</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>email_verified</code>, <code>userpool</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p>By default access tokens live 15 minutes and refresh tokens 14 days; change them in the pool's session policy.</p>
<p>Each time a refresh token is used, roles and scopes are read from the user's <b>current</b> record: access an administrator removes is gone
    from the next refreshed token. A suspended user cannot refresh.</p>

<h2 id="errors">Errors</h2>
<p>The <b>token endpoint</b> returns the standard OAuth 2.0 error body (RFC 6749): <code>error</code> (<code>invalid_request</code>,
    <code>invalid_client</code>, <code>invalid_grant</code>, <code>invalid_scope</code>, <code>invalid_target</code>, <code>unsupported_grant_type</code>),
    <code>error_description</code> (in the request language) and Booster Auth's own <code>code</code> (message key).</p>
<p>The <b>authorization endpoint</b> returns errors to the application's <code>redirect_uri</code> with <code>error</code> and <code>state</code>:
    <code>invalid_scope</code>, <code>invalid_target</code> (resource not allowed), <code>login_required</code> / <code>consent_required</code>
    (<code>prompt=none</code>), <code>access_denied</code> (the user denied on the consent screen). If the client or address cannot be validated, an
    error page is shown.</p>

<h2 id="consent">Consent screen</h2>
<p>If <b>Require user consent</b> is on for the application, the user sees the requested scopes at first sign-in and allows them; the consent is saved
    and not asked again for the same scopes. If the application asks for a new scope, the screen appears again. On deny the application gets
    <code>error=access_denied</code>. Consents can be revoked in the console on the application's <b>Consents</b> screen.</p>

<h2 id="service">Server to server (service account)</h2>
<p>For services that run without a human user, create a service account on the pool's <b>Service Accounts</b> screen; its key and secret are
    shown only once. The token is requested with Booster Auth's built-in <code>management</code> application; <code>resource</code> must be one of
    that application's allowed APIs. To grant roles / scopes to the pool's users and write relations with a service account, see
    <a href="/en/docs/pool-apis">Pool APIs</a>.</p>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">HTTP</span><pre>POST https://auth.company.com/idp/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials
&amp;client_id=management
&amp;service_account_key=SA_KEY
&amp;service_account_secret=SA_SECRET
&amp;resource=https://auth.company.com/idp</pre></div>
<p>The response contains only an access token; no ID token or refresh token is issued.</p>

<h2 id="hide-methods">Hiding sign-in methods</h2>
<p>The same application can show different sign-in options on different platforms; for example, no "Sign in with Apple" button in your
    Android app. Add the <code>hidden_providers</code> parameter to the <code>/idp/authorize</code> request:</p>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">Authorization request</span><pre>https://auth.company.com/idp/authorize?client_id=acme-mobile&amp;response_type=code
  &amp;redirect_uri=...&amp;scope=openid%20profile&amp;code_challenge=...&amp;code_challenge_method=S256
  &amp;hidden_providers=apple,phone_code</pre></div>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Hides</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>apple</code>, <code>google</code>, <code>microsoft</code>, <code>github</code>, ...</td><td>Every external identity provider button of that type</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A provider key (e.g. <code>corp-sso</code>)</td><td>Only that provider</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>email_code</code> / <code>phone_code</code></td><td>Passwordless sign-in with an e-mail / SMS code</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p>Values are separated by commas or spaces and are case-insensitive. The parameter only narrows the screen; it never enables a method that is
    turned off in the pool and is not a security rule.</p>

<h2 id="apple">Sign in with Apple</h2>
<p>Add Apple on the pool's <b>Identity Providers</b> screen:</p>
<ul>
    <li><b>client_id:</b> the <i>Services ID</i> from Apple Developer (e.g. <code>com.company.web</code>).</li>
    <li><b>team_id</b> and <b>key_id:</b> your Apple Developer team ID and the ID of the "Sign in with Apple" key.</li>
    <li><b>Secret key:</b> the contents of the downloaded <code>.p8</code> file (starts with <code>-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----</code>). Booster Auth
        uses it to generate the short-lived client secret Apple requires on every sign-in.</li>
    <li>Register <code>https://auth.company.com/idp/ui/external/callback</code> as the <i>Return URL</i> at Apple.</li>
</ul>
<p>Apple sends the user's name only on the first sign-in; Booster Auth stores it on the account. If the user hides their e-mail, an address
    ending in <code>@@privaterelay.appleid.com</code> is received.</p>

<h2 id="logout">Logout</h2>
<p>Redirect the user to <code>/idp/logout</code>; the session is closed and the user returns to the post-logout URI registered on the
    application. OIDC libraries do this with a call like <code>signoutRedirect()</code>.</p>
<p>To return to the application, the request must carry <code>client_id</code> or <code>id_token_hint</code> together with
    <code>post_logout_redirect_uri</code>. ASP.NET Core sends neither when <code>SaveTokens</code> is off; set
    <code>context.ProtocolMessage.ClientId</code> in the <code>OnRedirectToIdentityProviderForSignOut</code> event.</p>
<p><code>logout_all=true</code> ends <b>all</b> of the user's sessions; it is applied only together with an <code>id_token_hint</code> of that
    user (so another site cannot sign the user out everywhere). Without the hint only the current session ends.</p>
